{
  "decision": "ready",
  "reason": "The Plan's direction is well supported: P1 shows the actor only reached the box after a long repeat-walk and finally checking the coffeetable (P1:15-39), P3 never opened the fridge though it repeatedly stood there and never checked countertop 2/3, shelves, or the diningtable, and P6 cycled among empty drawers 1-4 without ever visiting sidetable 3, the sofa, armchair 2, or the tvstand. Broadening the search and reducing futile re-walking are therefore justified. However, both Updates are phrased as if the actor can recall the full set of locations it has already inspected, while the actor actually has only the current observation and the last two steps; Memory M3 and M6 also record that this memory-based anti-revisit language was not reliably followed and did not by itself break the cycling/step-limit failure. Both Updates are therefore kept in direction but reframed so their conditions and next-choice rule are executable from the current observation and immediate history.",
  "adjustments": [
    {
      "from": ["u1"],
      "to": [
        {
          "id": "u1",
          "text": "- When the exact requested target is not visible at the start, run one systematic first pass over the distinct receptacle types present in the current observation, not only over closed cabinets and drawers. Every closed container you reach (cabinet, drawer, fridge, microwave, safe, and similar) must actually be opened and its contents read before you leave it, rather than merely walked past or stood at. Also inspect open surfaces and appliance tops (coffeetable, diningtable, sidetable, countertop, dresser, shelf, sofa, armchair, ottoman, bed, stoveburner, sinkbasin, toaster, coffeemachine). Choose each next location from the currently admissible actions and the current observation, since you have only the current observation and the last two steps and cannot recall the whole visit history. Continue this pass until the exact target (or the required number of instances for a multi-target task) is found, or until every distinct receptacle type visible in the observation has been inspected once."
        }
      ],
      "basis": ["P1:36", "P3:6", "P3:15", "P3:19", "P3:26", "P6:7", "P6:21", "G1", "M3"],
      "reason": "P1 proves that the exact target can be plainly visible on an open surface (box on coffeetable 1, reached only at P1:36 after many wasted re-visits), and P3 repeatedly arrived at the fridge (P3:6, P3:15, P3:19) without ever opening it while also never checking countertop 2/3, the shelves, or the diningtable where the mug could have been; P6 similarly left sidetable 3, the sofa, armchair 2 and the tvstand unvisited. Memory M3 confirms added search breadth rescued the clean-dishsponge task (dishsponge found at the bathtubbasin only after broader exploration). The original wording also required the actor to 'prefer a candidate you have not yet observed', which presupposes a global visited-set the actor cannot maintain (history is only the last two steps); the revised text keeps the broad-search behavior but grounds candidate selection in the current observation and admissible actions and makes opening closed containers/appliances the operative requirement."
    },
    {
      "from": ["u2"],
      "to": [
        {
          "id": "u2",
          "text": "- Avoid unproductive re-walking using only the information available at the moment. If the receptacle you are about to choose is the one you just left, or the one you visited in the immediately preceding step, choose a different receptacle type from the current admissible actions instead. After opening a container and seeing that it does not hold the exact target, do not immediately reopen or re-examine that same container; move on to a different container or surface type. If consecutive steps only revisit containers that are already open and visibly empty, treat that as a stuck-search signal and switch to a receptacle type you have not yet checked in the current observation. Do not spend steps re-checking inventory while carrying nothing."
        }
      ],
      "basis": ["P1:15", "P1:20", "P6:22", "P6:36", "M3", "M6"],
      "reason": "P1 (P1:15-35) and P6 (P6:7-50) show the exact failure this Update targets: repeated returns to already-open empty cabinets/drawers. But the original rule asked the actor to avoid 'already-confirmed-empty' locations and to select ones absent from its 'recent observation/action history', which exceeds its two-step history, and Memory M6 records that this kind of memory-based anti-cycling clause 'was not reliably followed, cycling and step-limit failures persisted', while M3 likewise found the 'do not immediately revisit' clause did not by itself break cycling. The adjustment therefore changes strategy rather than repeating the unreliable instruction: detect and break cycling with the evidence the actor actually has (the last step and the current observation), and pick the next receptacle type from the current admissible actions."
    }
  ],
  "checks": null
}
